libwildlifesystems (1.3.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * ws_mock_reading_t gains a location token, so a driver's mock readings
    declare a location as sensor-onboard's already did and a mock has the
    same shape as a real read; the C drivers' mocks emitted location null.
    The token is parsed by the new ws_location_from_token(), which ws-emit now
    uses for --location in place of its own copy of the same code
  * ws-emit: a reading may carry its own --device, overriding the common one,
    and an empty name means it has none. sensor-onboard could not otherwise
    stop tagging storage_used and memory_used with raspberry_pi, which its
    own changelog says they must not be
  * ws-emit: without --prefix or --id, sensor_id is null rather than the bare
    sensor name. That name is the same on every node, so an id built from it
    collided across nodes; the C drivers already record an unknown id as null
  * The unit tests no longer depend on the build host. One test assumed the
    host had no /etc/geolocation and failed on any surveyed node, which is
    where the package is built; it now points GEOLOC_FILE away from the host.
    The negative sc-prototype test silences the "not found" it provokes, so
    a package build log does not show an error from a test that passed
  * Add a "make test" target that runs the unit tests, and have "make clean"
    clean them. The tests had no entry point from the library's Makefile, so
    nothing above it could run them
  * Every string placed in a reading by ws_build_sensor_json_base - sensor,
    device, measures, unit, sensor_id, sensor_name - is now escaped by the
    library. A sensor_id or sensor_name is typed into a config file, and a
    quote in either ended the JSON string early and invalidated the whole
    document, at which point sr dropped every reading from that driver. Only
    sensor-dht11 escaped anything, and only sensor_id; callers now pass strings
    raw, and the escaped copy is heap-allocated so a long value is refused
    whole rather than clipped
  * ws_sensor_json_set_error escapes the message in full. It went through a
    256-byte buffer, which silently cut anything longer; ws-emit takes --error
    from a command line of any length
  * ws_json_array_add treats an empty item as a failure of the array. No JSON
    value is empty, so one can only be a reading whose builder failed and
    cleared its buffer; two drivers appended it regardless and printed "[,]"
    with exit 0 whenever sc-prototype was unavailable
  * ws_require_prototype() confirms the template is available and logs if not,
    so a driver asks once before reading any hardware and fails with nothing
    printed. Nothing on stdout means "could not report"; "[]" would mean "no
    sensors". ws_cmd_mock and ws-emit already did this by hand; the three
    drivers did not
  * A surveyed accuracy is now emitted rather than discarded. It was read from
    both /etc/geolocation and a sensor's location config, validated, and
    stored, and then never rendered: a GeoJSON Point has nowhere to put it. A
    position is now a GeoJSON Feature wrapping that Point, with the accuracy
    radius in metres as properties.accuracy. A position known to two metres
    and one known to fifty are no longer indistinguishable
  * Every position takes that one shape, whether or not an accuracy was
    surveyed: properties.accuracy is null where none was, rather than the
    Feature collapsing back to a bare Point, so a consumer parses one form. This
    changes the location field of any reading that carries explicit coordinates
    or resolves "{{node}}". The "{{node}}" and "{{none}}" tokens and a null
    location are unaffected, each meaning something a position cannot express
  * The node position and a sensor's own position are rendered by one function
    rather than two copies of the same Point formatting, so the two cannot
    disagree about coordinate order or precision
  * Add ws_read_geolocation and ws_geolocation_geojson, reading the node's own
    position from /etc/geolocation in the GeoClue static-source format. Honours
    $GEOLOC_FILE, tolerates inline comments, stray whitespace, CRLF and
    fewer than four values, and treats a missing file as a normal state rather
    than an error
  * "{{node}}" now resolves on the node rather than being passed through for
    the server to dereference. A reading carries the position it was taken at,
    so moving and re-surveying a node does not retrospectively relocate its own
    history. Where the node has no usable location the token is emitted
    unresolved, keeping the declaration rather than discarding it
  * Add ws_cmd_usage, ws_cmd_unknown_arg and ws_arg_is_measurement, so a
    driver's usage line, the measurement filters it accepts and the measurements
    it lists all read from one array and cannot disagree. The hand-written usage
    strings had already drifted: sensor-bme680's omitted the four measurements
    its list command advertises, which is how it went unnoticed that it rejected
    all of them
  * Add ws_cmd_mock and ws_mock_reading_t. Each driver hand-rolled its "mock"
    command, and the copies had drifted: sensor-dht11 left its temperature
    sensor_id unsuffixed where a real read suffixes it, sensor-w1therm never got
    the missing-serial fallback the others have, and a missing template produced
    an empty array from two drivers and no output at all from the third. Drivers
    now give a table of values and the library builds the readings exactly as a
    real read does. A missing template fails with no output rather than an empty
    array that would read as "no sensors". Readings share one timestamp, as a
    real read does. An empty id_suffix means no suffix, for a driver with one
    measurement per sensor
  * Add ws_boot_config_path, ws_boot_config_has, ws_boot_config_add and
    ws_cmd_enable_boot_config. Editing the Pi boot config is how a driver's
    "enable" command makes its bus usable, and none of it is specific to one
    bus, but it lived in sensor-w1therm because 1-Wire needed it first.
    Directives are matched by prefix so a bus counts as enabled whatever
    parameters it was given, and an [all] section is added when the file has
    none, since a directive after a section such as [pi4] would apply to that
    model alone. $WS_BOOT_CONFIG_FILE overrides the path, which is how the tests
    cover this without a Pi
  * The boot config scan passes isspace() a value cast to unsigned char. A plain
    char is signed on some platforms, so a byte above 0x7f in a comment was
    undefined behaviour; harmless on the Pi, where char is unsigned
  * The JSON array builder now owns a heap buffer and grows it, rather than
    writing into a caller-supplied fixed one. Nothing used the fixed-buffer
    form, while every driver open-coded the same realloc-and-strcat loop, which
    re-measured the whole array per reading and, in sensor-dht11 and
    sensor-bme680, appended anyway when the realloc failed. Adds
    ws_json_array_free; ws_json_array_init takes no buffer and reports failure
  * Skip automatic -dbgsym generation, as the sensor drivers already do.
    ws-emit is the first executable this source ships, so dh_strip began
    producing a debug package that lintian reported as having no debug symbols:
    the Makefile sets CFLAGS itself rather than appending to dpkg-buildflags, so
    the binary carries none
  * Add ws-emit, in a new wildlifesystems-tools binary package: a command that
    turns readings described on its command line into reading JSON using this
    library. It lets sensor-onboard, which is shell, emit exactly what the C
    drivers emit instead of reimplementing the template in jq. Notably it gets
    "{{node}}" resolution, which the C drivers gained in this release and the
    shell driver could not have
  * Add WS_UNIT_CELSIUS, WS_UNIT_PERCENTAGE, WS_UNIT_HPA and WS_UNIT_OHMS, with
    ws_unit_canonical() to map a name to its canonical spelling case
    insensitively. A unit is part of a datastream's identity downstream, so a
    misspelling does not fail: it silently creates a second datastream for the
    same quantity. That has happened twice here, as "percant" and as "percent"
    where the other drivers said "percentage". ws-emit rejects an unknown unit
    rather than passing it through, which extends the protection to callers that
    are not C
  * The Makefile passed -DVERSION unquoted, so any use of VERSION in C failed to
    compile. Nothing had used it until ws-emit did
  * ws_sensor_config_base_t gains the location field and is now what drivers
    embed rather than redeclare. It had sat unused since 1.0 and had already
    fallen behind: when location was added it went into three private structs
    instead, and two of the six hand-rolled default-config blocks were missed,
    leaving an uninitialised read that could emit fabricated coordinates. A
    field added here now reaches every driver at once
  * Add ws_config_iter_open/next/close, replacing the read-count-allocate-walk
    sequence that each of the three drivers carried a line-for-line copy of.
    The iterator parses the common fields and hands back each entry's bounds so
    a driver parses only its own hardware-addressing field
  * Add ws_sensor_json_set_result, which sets either "value" or "error" and
    never both. sensor-w1therm was reporting the DS18B20 power-on sentinel
    (85.000) and the insufficient-power sentinel (127.937) in "value" next to
    the error naming them, so a consumer reading "value" without checking
    "error" ingested them as temperatures
  * ws_json_replace_null_string now takes the buffer capacity and refuses a
    value that will not fit, rather than formatting through a fixed 512-byte
    buffer. An over-long sensor_name or sensor_id was clipped mid-value, which
    left the JSON string unterminated and so invalidated the entire reading
    array, not merely that one field. ws_sensor_json_set_error and
    ws_sensor_json_set_result take the capacity for the same reason; the scalar
    replacers do not, their inserted text being of bounded length
  * ws_parse_sensor_location logs when a sensor declares no location, so an
    unconfigured sensor is distinguishable from one whose config was never
    read. Syslog only: it fires once per sensor per read cycle and would
    otherwise fill the per-driver stderr log
  * ws_get_serial_number trims trailing whitespace, not just the newline, so it
    agrees byte for byte with "pi-data serial" on any /proc/cpuinfo layout. sr
    takes node_id from pi-data while the drivers build sensor_id from this, so a
    disagreement would leave a reading whose sensor_id prefix did not match its
    own node_id
  * $WS_CPUINFO_FILE overrides the path, so the extraction is testable

 -- Ed Baker <edwbaker@gmail.com>  Sat, 12 Sep 2026 10:00:00 +0000

libwildlifesystems (1.2.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * Add ws_json_object_end for brace-matched object bounds
  * Add ws_json_parse_object for nested JSON object fields
  * Add ws_json_parse_double for floating-point JSON fields
  * ws_json_count_objects now counts top-level objects only,
    ignoring nesting and braces inside string literals
  * ws_json_parse_string now requires the value to actually be a string, and
    honours \" escapes. Previously an object-valued field returned the first
    key inside it, and a value containing an escaped quote was truncated
  * Add ws_location_t, ws_parse_sensor_location and ws_location_json for
    per-sensor locations from /etc/ws/sensors/<driver>.json
  * Add ws_json_replace_null_raw for object and pre-quoted field values;
    ws_sensor_json_set_config now uses it
  * ws_build_sensor_json_base takes device and location parameters.
    API change: all callers must be updated and rebuilt
  * Add unit tests for the new JSON parser functions, location parsing and
    raw field replacement

 -- Ed Baker <edwbaker@gmail.com>  Fri, 11 Sep 2026 10:00:00 +0000

